.TH AGENT-SANDBOX-EXEC 1 "2026-07-01" "agent-sandbox-exec 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
agent-sandbox-exec \- run a command inside the BPF-LSM agent sandbox
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B agent-sandbox-exec
.RB [ -h ]
.RB [ -V ]
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B agent-sandbox-exec
runs
.I command
(and its arguments) inside the agent sandbox: the process and every process it
spawns is migrated into a per-uid agent cgroup
.RI ( /sys/fs/cgroup/agent-sandbox-exec/uid-<uid> ),
where a BPF-LSM program on the
.I file_open
hook blocks opens of the files listed in the denylist, returning
.BR ENOENT .
.PP
The effective denylist for a uid is the union of the root-controlled base list
.RI ( /etc/agent-sandbox-exec/denylist )
and that uid's home list
.RI ( ~/.config/agent-sandbox-exec/denylist ).
A user-controlled home list is safe because deny entries are scoped to the
user's own cgroup: they can only block opens inside that user's own sandbox.
.PP
This defeats read-via-delegation \(em
.BR "cat secret | \&..." ,
hardlinks to a secret, and
.B "cp secret"
are all blocked, because the source
.B open
is denied.
.PP
No mount or user namespace is created, so the command sees the real environment
\(em real mount table, real
.I /dev
ownership, real supplementary groups, real
.I /proc
\(em and can still troubleshoot kernel and system issues accurately.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h , " \-\-help"
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V , " \-\-version"
Print version and exit.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
The command ran and exited 0.
.TP
.B 2
The sandbox was not ready, migration timed out (is
.B agent-sandbox-execd
running?), or no command was given. The command was
.I not
executed.
.TP
.I *
Otherwise the command's own exit status.
.SH "FAIL-CLOSED BEHAVIOUR"
Fail-closed is unconditional. If
.B agent-sandbox-execd
is not running, the agent cgroup is absent, or migration does not complete, the
command is
.I not
executed (exit 2). There is no escape-hatch environment variable: to run a
command without the sandbox, invoke it directly rather than through this
wrapper.
.PP
A uid's denylist is loaded on first launch and then
.I frozen
until
.B agent-sandbox-execd
is restarted. To apply denylist changes (base or home), restart the daemon
.RB ( "systemctl restart agent-sandbox-execd" ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/agent-sandbox-exec/denylist
Root-controlled base denylist, applied to every sandboxed uid \(em see
.BR agent-sandbox-exec-denylist (5).
.TP
.I ~/.config/agent-sandbox-exec/denylist
Per-user home denylist (NFS-safe); unioned with the base list for that uid.
.TP
.I /sys/fs/cgroup/agent-sandbox-exec
Parent cgroup (root-owned, not delegated); per-uid children
.I uid-<uid>
hold the sandboxed processes.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR agent-sandbox-exec-denylist (5)